/*
 * The `transforms` module's passes.
 *
 * `reinterp` is the forward replacement of a program's primitives by the rules of a ruleset
 * family (`docs/design/program-transforms/README.cn.md` 4.1). Its first form builds the
 * transform's own graph and says what it built; the numbers it will have to compute are
 * `f(2) = 5.5` and `f'(2) = 3.25` for `f(x) = x*x + 3/x`.
 *
 * The module's other half is the value-level transform `jvp(f)` -- `reinterp<jvp>(f)` as a call,
 * not a pass: it answers with a new function, which a pass cannot (modules/transforms/jvp.h).
 * Its signature is computed from the argument, so it is recorded in
 * `stdlib/signatures/transforms.cml`.
 */

/// Builds the transform's own graph for every function of the program that is made of operators and
/// prints what it built, one line per function:
/// `REINTERP target=<graph name> primitives=<n> other=<n> graph=<id> nodes=<n>`. The primitives are
/// the operator nodes a replacement has to replace; `other` is everything else in the function. The
/// graph is the transform's own (a member of one draft group), not the function's.
///
///     camel f.cml transforms::reinterp std::nvm
export func reinterp::<F>(f: F): F! when is_function::<F>()